"""CMO: the precise textbook statement of each card's theorem / definition / algorithm (shown above THE MATH).
Standard formulations (Luenberger & Ye, Nocedal & Wright, Boyd & Vandenberghe style), written out in full."""

DEF, THM, ALG, PROP, LEM, EX, COR = "DEFINITION", "THEOREM", "ALGORITHM", "PROPOSITION", "LEMMA", "WORKED EXAMPLE", "COROLLARY"

ST = {
  # ---- 1 · Preliminaries ----
  101: (THM, r"<b>Heine–Borel.</b> A subset $K\subset\mathbb R^n$ is compact (every sequence in $K$ has a subsequence converging to a point of $K$) if and only if $K$ is closed and bounded. Here <i>closed</i> means $K$ contains all its limit points, and <i>bounded</i> means $K\subset\{x:\|x\|\le R\}$ for some $R<\infty$."),
  102: (DEF, r"A function $f:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R\cup\{+\infty\}$ is <b>lower semicontinuous</b> at $x_0$ if $\liminf_{x\to x_0}f(x)\ge f(x_0)$. <b>Proposition.</b> $f$ is lower semicontinuous on $\mathbb R^n$ if and only if every sublevel set $\mathcal L_\alpha=\{x: f(x)\le\alpha\}$, $\alpha\in\mathbb R$, is closed."),
  103: (THM, r"<b>Weierstrass.</b> Let $K\subset\mathbb R^n$ be nonempty and compact, and let $f:K\to\mathbb R$ be continuous (lower semicontinuous suffices). Then $f$ attains its minimum on $K$: there exists $x^*\in K$ with $f(x^*)\le f(x)$ for all $x\in K$."),
  104: (THM, r"Let $f:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R$ be continuous and <b>coercive</b>, i.e. $f(x)\to+\infty$ whenever $\|x\|\to\infty$. Then every sublevel set $\{x:f(x)\le\alpha\}$ is compact, and $f$ has a global minimizer on $\mathbb R^n$."),
  105: (DEF, r"The <b>epigraph</b> of $f:C\to\mathbb R$ is $\operatorname{epi}f=\{(x,t)\in C\times\mathbb R: f(x)\le t\}$. <b>Proposition.</b> For a convex set $C$, $f$ is convex on $C$ if and only if $\operatorname{epi}f$ is a convex subset of $\mathbb R^{n+1}$."),
  106: (EX, r"Let $f(x)=x_1^4+x_2^4-4x_1x_2$. Since $4|x_1x_2|\le2(x_1^2+x_2^2)$, $f(x)\ge x_1^4+x_2^4-2(x_1^2+x_2^2)\to\infty$ as $\|x\|\to\infty$, so $f$ is coercive and a global minimizer exists. Solving $\nabla f=0$ gives $(0,0),(1,1),(-1,-1)$; the global minimum is $f(\pm(1,1))=-2$."),
  # ---- 2 · Taylor & optimality ----
  201: (THM, r"<b>Taylor (second order).</b> Let $f\in C^2$ on an open set containing the segment $[x,x+d]$. Then there exists $\theta\in(0,1)$ such that $f(x+d)=f(x)+\nabla f(x)^\top d+\tfrac12 d^\top\nabla^2f(x+\theta d)\,d$. Equivalently, $f(x+d)=f(x)+\nabla f(x)^\top d+\tfrac12d^\top\nabla^2f(x)d+o(\|d\|^2)$."),
  202: (THM, r"<b>First-order necessary condition.</b> Let $f\in C^1$ on an open set $\Omega$. If $x^*\in\Omega$ is a local minimizer of $f$, then $\nabla f(x^*)=0$."),
  203: (THM, r"<b>Second-order necessary conditions.</b> Let $f\in C^2$ on an open set $\Omega$. If $x^*\in\Omega$ is a local minimizer of $f$, then $\nabla f(x^*)=0$ and $\nabla^2f(x^*)$ is positive semidefinite: $d^\top\nabla^2f(x^*)d\ge0$ for all $d$."),
  204: (THM, r"<b>Second-order sufficient conditions.</b> Let $f\in C^2$ on an open set $\Omega$ and $x^*\in\Omega$. If $\nabla f(x^*)=0$ and $\nabla^2f(x^*)$ is positive definite, then $x^*$ is a strict local minimizer of $f$."),
  205: (PROP, r"Let $x^*$ be a critical point ($\nabla f(x^*)=0$) of $f\in C^2$ with Hessian eigenvalues $\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_n$. If all $\lambda_i>0$, $x^*$ is a strict local minimizer; if all $\lambda_i<0$, a strict local maximizer; if some $\lambda_i>0$ and some $\lambda_j<0$, $x^*$ is a saddle point. If the Hessian is singular and semidefinite, the test is inconclusive."),
  206: (EX, r"For Rosenbrock's $f(x)=100(x_2-x_1^2)^2+(1-x_1)^2$: $\nabla f(1,1)=0$ and $\nabla^2f(1,1)=\begin{bmatrix}802&-400\\-400&200\end{bmatrix}$ with $\det=400>0$ and $\operatorname{tr}=1002>0$, so both eigenvalues are positive and $(1,1)$ is a strict local (indeed global) minimizer, with $\kappa\approx2500$."),
  # ---- 3 · Convexity ----
  301: (DEF, r"A set $C$ is <b>convex</b> if $\theta x+(1-\theta)y\in C$ for all $x,y\in C$, $\theta\in[0,1]$. A function $f:C\to\mathbb R$ on a convex set is <b>convex</b> if $f(\theta x+(1-\theta)y)\le\theta f(x)+(1-\theta)f(y)$ for all $x,y\in C$, $\theta\in[0,1]$; <b>strictly convex</b> if the inequality is strict whenever $x\ne y$, $\theta\in(0,1)$."),
  302: (THM, r"Let $f\in C^1$ on an open convex set $C$. Then $f$ is convex on $C$ if and only if $f(y)\ge f(x)+\nabla f(x)^\top(y-x)$ for all $x,y\in C$."),
  303: (THM, r"Let $f\in C^2$ on an open convex set $C$. Then $f$ is convex on $C$ if and only if $\nabla^2f(x)$ is positive semidefinite for every $x\in C$. (If $\nabla^2f(x)\succ0$ for all $x\in C$, then $f$ is strictly convex; the converse fails, e.g. $x^4$.)"),
  304: (DEF, r"$f$ is <b>$\mu$-strongly convex</b> ($\mu>0$) if $f(x)-\tfrac\mu2\|x\|^2$ is convex. <b>Equivalently</b> (for $f\in C^1$): $f(y)\ge f(x)+\nabla f(x)^\top(y-x)+\tfrac\mu2\|y-x\|^2$ for all $x,y$; and (for $f\in C^2$): $\nabla^2f(x)\succeq\mu I$ for all $x$."),
  305: (DEF, r"$f$ with minimum value $f^*$ satisfies the <b>Polyak–Łojasiewicz inequality</b> with constant $\mu>0$ if $\tfrac12\|\nabla f(x)\|^2\ge\mu\,(f(x)-f^*)$ for all $x$. <b>Theorem.</b> If moreover $\nabla f$ is $L$-Lipschitz, gradient descent with step $1/L$ satisfies $f(x_k)-f^*\le(1-\mu/L)^k(f(x_0)-f^*)$."),
  306: (PROP, r"For $f\in C^2$ on a convex set: $\mu$-strongly convex ($\nabla^2f\succeq\mu I$, $\mu>0$) $\Rightarrow$ strictly convex $\Rightarrow$ convex ($\nabla^2f\succeq0$). Neither implication reverses: $x^4$ is strictly but not strongly convex, and a linear function is convex but not strictly convex."),
  # ---- 4 · Line search ----
  401: (DEF, r"A <b>line search method</b> generates $x_{k+1}=x_k+\alpha_kd_k$, where $d_k$ is a <b>descent direction</b>, $\nabla f(x_k)^\top d_k<0$, and the step length $\alpha_k>0$ is chosen by a line search along $d_k$."),
  402: (PROP, r"For $f(x)=\tfrac12x^\top Qx-b^\top x$ with $Q\succ0$ and a descent direction $d_k$, the exact minimizer of $\phi(\alpha)=f(x_k+\alpha d_k)$ is $\alpha_k^*=-\dfrac{\nabla f(x_k)^\top d_k}{d_k^\top Qd_k}$, and at $x_{k+1}=x_k+\alpha_k^*d_k$ one has $\nabla f(x_{k+1})^\top d_k=0$."),
  403: (DEF, r"<b>Armijo (sufficient decrease) condition.</b> Given $c_1\in(0,1)$, a step $\alpha>0$ along a descent direction $d_k$ satisfies the Armijo condition if $f(x_k+\alpha d_k)\le f(x_k)+c_1\alpha\nabla f(x_k)^\top d_k$."),
  404: (DEF, r"<b>Wolfe conditions.</b> With $0<c_1<c_2<1$, a step $\alpha_k$ satisfies the Wolfe conditions if (i) $f(x_k+\alpha_kd_k)\le f(x_k)+c_1\alpha_k\nabla f(x_k)^\top d_k$ and (ii) $\nabla f(x_k+\alpha_kd_k)^\top d_k\ge c_2\nabla f(x_k)^\top d_k$. The <b>strong Wolfe</b> conditions replace (ii) by $|\nabla f(x_k+\alpha_kd_k)^\top d_k|\le c_2|\nabla f(x_k)^\top d_k|$."),
  405: (THM, r"<b>Zoutendijk.</b> Let $f$ be bounded below and $C^1$ with $\nabla f$ Lipschitz on a neighbourhood of the level set $\{f\le f(x_0)\}$. For any iteration $x_{k+1}=x_k+\alpha_kd_k$ with descent directions $d_k$ and steps satisfying the Wolfe conditions, $\sum_k\cos^2\theta_k\|\nabla f(x_k)\|^2<\infty$, where $\cos\theta_k=\dfrac{-\nabla f(x_k)^\top d_k}{\|\nabla f(x_k)\|\|d_k\|}$. Hence if $\cos\theta_k\ge\delta>0$ for all $k$, then $\|\nabla f(x_k)\|\to0$."),
  406: (ALG, r"<b>Backtracking line search.</b> Choose $\bar\alpha>0$, $\rho\in(0,1)$, $c_1\in(0,1)$; set $\alpha\leftarrow\bar\alpha$. While $f(x_k+\alpha d_k)>f(x_k)+c_1\alpha\nabla f(x_k)^\top d_k$, set $\alpha\leftarrow\rho\alpha$. Return $\alpha_k=\alpha$. For a descent direction this terminates after finitely many reductions."),
  # ---- 5 · Gradient descent ----
  501: (ALG, r"<b>Steepest descent.</b> Given $x_0$ and tolerance $\varepsilon$: for $k=0,1,\dots$ set $d_k=-\nabla f(x_k)$, choose $\alpha_k>0$ by a line search, and set $x_{k+1}=x_k-\alpha_k\nabla f(x_k)$; stop when $\|\nabla f(x_k)\|\le\varepsilon\|\nabla f(x_0)\|$."),
  508: (PROP, r"For $f(x)=\tfrac12x^\top Qx-b^\top x$, $Q\succ0$, steepest descent with exact line search uses $\alpha_k=\dfrac{g_k^\top g_k}{g_k^\top Qg_k}$ (in general $\alpha_k=\dfrac{-g_k^\top u_k}{u_k^\top Qu_k}$ for direction $u_k$), and successive gradients are orthogonal: $g_{k+1}^\top g_k=0$, i.e. $(x_{k+2}-x_{k+1})^\top(x_{k+1}-x_k)=0$."),
  502: (THM, r"Let $f$ be convex and differentiable with $L$-Lipschitz gradient, and let $x^*$ be a minimizer. Gradient descent with constant step $1/L$ satisfies $f(x_k)-f^*\le\dfrac{L\|x_0-x^*\|^2}{2k}$ for all $k\ge1$."),
  503: (THM, r"Let $f$ be $\mu$-strongly convex with $L$-Lipschitz gradient, $\kappa=L/\mu$. Gradient descent with step $\alpha=\dfrac{2}{\mu+L}$ satisfies $\|x_k-x^*\|\le\left(\dfrac{\kappa-1}{\kappa+1}\right)^k\|x_0-x^*\|$."),
  504: (THM, r"<b>Kantorovich inequality.</b> If $Q\succ0$ has smallest and largest eigenvalues $a$ and $A$, then $\dfrac{(x^\top x)^2}{(x^\top Qx)(x^\top Q^{-1}x)}\ge\dfrac{4aA}{(a+A)^2}$ for all $x\ne0$. <b>Corollary.</b> Steepest descent with exact line search on $\tfrac12x^\top Qx-b^\top x$ satisfies $E(x_{k+1})\le\left(\dfrac{A-a}{A+a}\right)^2E(x_k)$, where $E(x)=\tfrac12(x-x^*)^\top Q(x-x^*)$."),
  505: (DEF, r"The <b>condition number</b> of $Q\succ0$ is $\kappa=\lambda_{\max}(Q)/\lambda_{\min}(Q)\ge1$. The steepest-descent rate factor $\left(\frac{\kappa-1}{\kappa+1}\right)^2\to1$ as $\kappa\to\infty$, so convergence stagnates on ill-conditioned problems."),
  506: (DEF, r"<b>Barzilai–Borwein steps.</b> With $s_k=x_k-x_{k-1}$ and $y_k=\nabla f(x_k)-\nabla f(x_{k-1})$, the gradient iteration $x_{k+1}=x_k-\alpha_k\nabla f(x_k)$ uses $\alpha_k^{BB1}=\dfrac{s_k^\top s_k}{s_k^\top y_k}$ or $\alpha_k^{BB2}=\dfrac{s_k^\top y_k}{y_k^\top y_k}$; these solve $\min_\alpha\|\alpha^{-1}s_k-y_k\|$ and $\min_\alpha\|s_k-\alpha y_k\|$ respectively."),
  507: (DEF, r"<b>Heavy-ball method.</b> $x_{k+1}=x_k-\alpha\nabla f(x_k)+\beta(x_k-x_{k-1})$. For a strongly convex quadratic with optimally tuned $\alpha,\beta$ it converges at rate $\dfrac{\sqrt\kappa-1}{\sqrt\kappa+1}$, compared with $\dfrac{\kappa-1}{\kappa+1}$ for gradient descent."),
  # ---- 6 · Newton ----
  601: (DEF, r"<b>Newton's method.</b> Given $x_k$ with $\nabla^2f(x_k)$ nonsingular, compute the Newton step $p_k$ from $\nabla^2f(x_k)p_k=-\nabla f(x_k)$ and set $x_{k+1}=x_k+p_k$. Equivalently, $x_{k+1}$ minimizes (or is a critical point of) the quadratic model $m_k(p)=f(x_k)+\nabla f(x_k)^\top p+\tfrac12p^\top\nabla^2f(x_k)p$."),
  602: (THM, r"Let $f\in C^2$ with $\nabla^2f$ Lipschitz (constant $L$) near a point $x^*$ where $\nabla f(x^*)=0$ and $\nabla^2f(x^*)\succeq mI\succ0$. If $x_0$ is sufficiently close to $x^*$, Newton's method converges to $x^*$ quadratically: $\|x_{k+1}-x^*\|\le\dfrac{L}{2m}\|x_k-x^*\|^2$."),
  603: (PROP, r"The Newton step is a descent direction, $p_k^\top\nabla f(x_k)<0$, whenever $\nabla^2f(x_k)\succ0$ and $\nabla f(x_k)\ne0$. If $\nabla^2f(x_k)$ is not positive definite, $p_k$ may fail to exist or may satisfy $p_k^\top\nabla f(x_k)\ge0$, and Newton's method may converge to a saddle point or maximizer."),
  604: (ALG, r"<b>Damped Newton.</b> Compute $p_k$ from $\nabla^2f(x_k)p_k=-\nabla f(x_k)$, choose $\alpha_k=\beta^m$ ($\beta\in(0,1)$) with $m\ge0$ the smallest integer satisfying the Armijo condition, and set $x_{k+1}=x_k+\alpha_kp_k$. If $c_1\in(0,\tfrac12)$ and the iterates approach a point satisfying the second-order sufficient conditions, eventually $\alpha_k=1$ and convergence is quadratic."),
  605: (DEF, r"<b>Levenberg–Marquardt / Hessian modification.</b> Solve $(\nabla^2f(x_k)+\tau_kI)p_k=-\nabla f(x_k)$ with $\tau_k\ge\max\{0,\,-\lambda_{\min}(\nabla^2f(x_k))+\delta\}$, $\delta>0$. Then $\nabla^2f(x_k)+\tau_kI\succ0$ and $p_k$ is a descent direction; $\tau_k\to0$ recovers Newton, $\tau_k\to\infty$ gives a short steepest-descent step $p_k\approx-\nabla f(x_k)/\tau_k$."),
  606: (EX, r"For Rosenbrock's $f(x)=100(x_2-x_1^2)^2+(1-x_1)^2$ at $(0,0)$: $\nabla f=(-2,0)^\top$, $\nabla^2f=\operatorname{diag}(2,200)$, so $\kappa=100$ and the Newton step is $p_N=-H^{-1}g=(1,0)^\top$, heading straight along the canyon floor, whereas a gradient step is slowed by $\kappa$."),
  # ---- 7 · CG ----
  701: (DEF, r"Given $Q\succ0$, nonzero vectors $d_0,\dots,d_{k}$ are <b>$Q$-conjugate</b> if $d_i^\top Qd_j=0$ for all $i\ne j$; they are then linearly independent. The <b>conjugate direction method</b> for $\tfrac12x^\top Qx-b^\top x$ sets $x_{k+1}=x_k+\alpha_kd_k$ with $\alpha_k=-\dfrac{g_k^\top d_k}{d_k^\top Qd_k}$, $g_k=Qx_k-b=-r_k$, and reaches $x^*$ in at most $n$ steps."),
  702: (THM, r"<b>Expanding subspace theorem.</b> Let $d_0,\dots,d_{n-1}$ be $Q$-conjugate and $x_k$ generated by the conjugate direction method from $x_0$. Then $x_k$ minimizes $f(x)=\tfrac12x^\top Qx-b^\top x$ on the affine set $x_0+\mathcal B_k$, $\mathcal B_k=\operatorname{span}\{d_0,\dots,d_{k-1}\}$, and $g_k^\top d_i=0$ for all $i<k$."),
  703: (ALG, r"<b>Conjugate gradient (Fletcher–Reeves form).</b> Set $d_0=-g_0$. For $k=0,1,\dots$: $\alpha_k=\dfrac{g_k^\top g_k}{d_k^\top Qd_k}$, $x_{k+1}=x_k+\alpha_kd_k$, $g_{k+1}=g_k+\alpha_kQd_k$, $\beta_k=\dfrac{g_{k+1}^\top g_{k+1}}{g_k^\top g_k}$, $d_{k+1}=-g_{k+1}+\beta_kd_k$. The directions $d_k$ are $Q$-conjugate."),
  704: (THM, r"In conjugate gradient, $\mathcal B_k=\operatorname{span}\{d_0,\dots,d_{k-1}\}=\operatorname{span}\{g_0,\dots,g_{k-1}\}=\mathcal K_k(Q,r_0)=\operatorname{span}\{r_0,Qr_0,\dots,Q^{k-1}r_0\}$. If $Q$ has $m$ distinct eigenvalues, CG reaches $x^*$ in at most $m\le n$ iterations (in exact arithmetic)."),
  705: (DEF, r"<b>Nonlinear CG (Polak–Ribière).</b> For general $f$, use $d_{k+1}=-g_{k+1}+\beta_kd_k$ with $\beta_k^{PR}=\dfrac{g_{k+1}^\top(g_{k+1}-g_k)}{\|g_k\|^2}$ and step $\alpha_k$ from a line search. The <b>PR+</b> variant uses $\beta_k^+=\max\{0,\beta_k^{PR}\}$, which restarts with steepest descent whenever $\beta_k^{PR}<0$."),
  706: (EX, r"Minimize $\tfrac12x^\top Qx$ with $Q\succ0$ in $\mathbb R^2$ from $x_0=(4,1)$: CG takes $\alpha_0=1/5$ to $x_1=(2.4,-0.6)$, then $\beta_0=0.36$, $\alpha_1=5/16$ to $x_2=(0,0)=x^*$, terminating in $n=2$ steps as guaranteed."),
  # ---- 8 · Quasi-Newton ----
  801: (DEF, r"With $s_k=x_{k+1}-x_k$ and $y_k=\nabla f(x_{k+1})-\nabla f(x_k)$, a Hessian approximation $B_{k+1}$ satisfies the <b>secant equation</b> if $B_{k+1}s_k=y_k$ (equivalently $H_{k+1}y_k=s_k$ for $H=B^{-1}$). A symmetric positive definite solution exists iff the curvature condition $s_k^\top y_k>0$ holds."),
  802: (DEF, r"<b>Symmetric rank-one (SR1) update.</b> $B_{k+1}=B_k+\dfrac{(y_k-B_ks_k)(y_k-B_ks_k)^\top}{(y_k-B_ks_k)^\top s_k}$. It is the unique symmetric rank-one correction satisfying the secant equation; it is undefined when the denominator vanishes and need not preserve positive definiteness."),
  803: (DEF, r"<b>DFP update</b> (of the inverse Hessian approximation $H_k$): $H_{k+1}=H_k-\dfrac{H_ky_ky_k^\top H_k}{y_k^\top H_ky_k}+\dfrac{s_ks_k^\top}{y_k^\top s_k}$. It satisfies $H_{k+1}y_k=s_k$ and preserves positive definiteness when $y_k^\top s_k>0$."),
  804: (DEF, r"<b>BFGS update.</b> $B_{k+1}=B_k-\dfrac{B_ks_ks_k^\top B_k}{s_k^\top B_ks_k}+\dfrac{y_ky_k^\top}{y_k^\top s_k}$, equivalently $H_{k+1}=(I-\rho_ks_ky_k^\top)H_k(I-\rho_ky_ks_k^\top)+\rho_ks_ks_k^\top$ with $\rho_k=1/(y_k^\top s_k)$. It is the dual of DFP (swap $s\leftrightarrow y$, $B\leftrightarrow H$) and is self-correcting in practice."),
  805: (THM, r"If $B_k\succ0$ and $s_k^\top y_k>0$, then the BFGS matrix $B_{k+1}$ is positive definite. If $\alpha_k$ satisfies the Wolfe conditions, then $s_k^\top y_k\ge(c_2-1)\alpha_k\nabla f(x_k)^\top d_k>0$, so positive definiteness is inherited at every iteration."),
  806: (ALG, r"<b>L-BFGS.</b> Store only the $m$ most recent pairs $\{(s_i,y_i)\}_{i=k-m}^{k-1}$. Compute $d_k=-H_k\nabla f(x_k)$ by the two-loop recursion, applying the $m$ BFGS updates to an initial matrix $H_k^0=\gamma_kI$ (e.g. $\gamma_k=s_{k-1}^\top y_{k-1}/y_{k-1}^\top y_{k-1}$), at cost $O(mn)$ per iteration; discard the oldest pair after each step."),
  # ---- 9 · Tangent cones ----
  901: (DEF, r"For $\Omega=\{x: c_i(x)=0,\ i\in\mathcal E;\ c_i(x)\le0,\ i\in\mathcal I\}$, the <b>active set</b> at a feasible $x$ is $\mathcal A(x)=\mathcal E\cup\{i\in\mathcal I: c_i(x)=0\}$. An inequality with $c_i(x)\lt0$ is <b>inactive</b>."),
  902: (DEF, r"A vector $d$ is <b>tangent</b> to $\Omega$ at $x$ if there are feasible $z_k\to x$ and $t_k\downarrow0$ with $\lim_k\dfrac{z_k-x}{t_k}=d$. For a smooth surface $\{h(x)=0\}$ the tangent vectors at a regular point form the <b>tangent plane</b> $M=\{d:\nabla h(x)^\top d=0\}$."),
  903: (DEF, r"The <b>tangent cone</b> $T_\Omega(x)$ is the set of all tangent vectors to $\Omega$ at $x$. It is a closed cone ($d\in T_\Omega(x)\Rightarrow\lambda d\in T_\Omega(x)$ for $\lambda\ge0$), and for convex $\Omega$ it is the closure of the cone of feasible directions."),
  905: (THM, r"<b>Geometric first-order condition.</b> If $x^*$ is a local minimizer of $f\in C^1$ over $\Omega$, then $\nabla f(x^*)^\top d\ge0$ for all $d\in T_\Omega(x^*)$."),
  906: (DEF, r"The <b>linearized cone</b> is $\mathcal F(x)=\{d:\nabla c_i(x)^\top d=0,\ i\in\mathcal E;\ \nabla c_i(x)^\top d\le0,\ i\in\mathcal A(x)\cap\mathcal I\}$. Always $T_\Omega(x)\subseteq\mathcal F(x)$. <b>LICQ</b> holds at $x$ if $\{\nabla c_i(x)\}_{i\in\mathcal A(x)}$ is linearly independent, and then $T_\Omega(x)=\mathcal F(x)$."),
  # ---- 10 · Separation & Farkas ----
  1001: (THM, r"<b>Separating hyperplane.</b> Let $C,D\subset\mathbb R^n$ be nonempty disjoint convex sets. Then there exist $a\ne0$ and $b$ with $a^\top x\le b$ for all $x\in C$ and $a^\top x\ge b$ for all $x\in D$. If $C$ is closed and $D$ is compact, the separation can be made strict."),
  1002: (THM, r"<b>Supporting hyperplane.</b> Let $C$ be convex and $x_0$ a boundary point of $C$. Then there exists $a\ne0$ with $a^\top x\le a^\top x_0$ for all $x\in C$."),
  1003: (LEM, r"<b>Farkas.</b> Let $A\in\mathbb R^{m\times n}$, $b\in\mathbb R^m$. Exactly one of the following holds: (i) there is $x\ge0$ with $Ax=b$; (ii) there is $y$ with $A^\top y\ge0$ and $b^\top y<0$."),
  1007: (EX, r"With columns $a_1=(1,0)$, $a_2=(1,1)$, is $b'=(0,1)$ in the cone $\{x_1a_1+x_2a_2:x\ge0\}$? No: $y=(1,-1)$ gives $a_1^\top y=1\ge0$, $a_2^\top y=0\ge0$ and $b'^\top y=-1<0$, so alternative (ii) of Farkas' lemma holds."),
  1004: (THM, r"<b>Gordan.</b> For $A\in\mathbb R^{m\times n}$, exactly one holds: (i) there is $x$ with $Ax<0$ (componentwise); (ii) there is $y\ge0$, $y\ne0$, with $A^\top y=0$."),
  1005: (DEF, r"For a cone $K$, the <b>dual cone</b> is $K^*=\{y: y^\top x\ge0\ \forall x\in K\}$ and the <b>polar cone</b> is $K^\circ=\{y: y^\top x\le0\ \forall x\in K\}=-K^*$. For a closed convex cone, $K^{**}=K$."),
  1006: (THM, r"<b>KKT via Farkas.</b> Let $x^*$ be a local minimizer at which $T_\Omega(x^*)=\mathcal F(x^*)$ (e.g. under LICQ). Then $\nabla f(x^*)^\top d\ge0$ for all $d\in\mathcal F(x^*)$, and Farkas' lemma yields multipliers $\lambda_i$ ($\lambda_i\ge0$ for active inequalities) with $\nabla f(x^*)+\sum_{i\in\mathcal A(x^*)}\lambda_i\nabla c_i(x^*)=0$."),
  # ---- 11 · KKT ----
  1101: (THM, r"<b>KKT necessary conditions.</b> Consider $\min f(x)$ s.t. $h_i(x)=0$, $g_j(x)\le0$, all $C^1$. If $x^*$ is a local minimizer and LICQ holds at $x^*$, there exist $\lambda,\mu$ with: (stationarity) $\nabla f(x^*)+\sum_i\lambda_i\nabla h_i(x^*)+\sum_j\mu_j\nabla g_j(x^*)=0$; (primal feasibility) $h(x^*)=0$, $g(x^*)\le0$; (dual feasibility) $\mu\ge0$; (complementary slackness) $\mu_jg_j(x^*)=0$ for all $j$."),
  1102: (DEF, r"<b>Complementary slackness.</b> At a KKT point, $\mu_jg_j(x^*)=0$ for each $j$: either $g_j(x^*)=0$ (constraint active) or $\mu_j=0$. It is <b>strict</b> if exactly one of $\mu_j$, $g_j(x^*)$ is zero for every $j$."),
  1103: (THM, r"<b>KKT sufficiency.</b> If $f$ and every $g_j$ are convex, every $h_i$ is affine, and $(x^*,\lambda,\mu)$ satisfies the KKT conditions, then $x^*$ is a global minimizer."),
  1104: (DEF, r"<b>LICQ.</b> The gradients $\{\nabla h_i(x^*)\}_i\cup\{\nabla g_j(x^*): g_j(x^*)=0\}$ are linearly independent. Under LICQ the KKT multipliers at a local minimizer exist and are unique; without a constraint qualification a minimizer need not satisfy KKT."),
  1105: (THM, r"<b>Slater.</b> For a convex program ($f,g_j$ convex, $h$ affine), if there is $\bar x$ with $g_j(\bar x)<0$ for all $j$ and $h(\bar x)=0$, then (i) KKT conditions are necessary at any minimizer, and (ii) strong duality holds: $p^*=d^*$, with the dual optimum attained."),
  1106: (EX, r"KKT case analysis: assume a set of active constraints, solve the stationarity and active equations, then check primal feasibility ($g\le0$) and dual feasibility ($\mu\ge0$). The case that survives both checks (here: the constraint active with $\mu^*>0$) is the KKT point."),
  # ---- 12 · Second-order constrained ----
  1201: (THM, r"<b>Second-order necessary conditions.</b> Let $x^*$ be a local minimizer with LICQ and KKT multipliers $\lambda^*$. Then $d^\top\nabla_{xx}^2L(x^*,\lambda^*)d\ge0$ for all $d$ in the tangent subspace $M=\{d:\nabla c_i(x^*)^\top d=0,\ i\in\mathcal A(x^*)\}$ (for strictly complementary multipliers)."),
  1202: (THM, r"<b>Second-order sufficient conditions.</b> If $(x^*,\lambda^*)$ satisfies KKT and $d^\top\nabla_{xx}^2L(x^*,\lambda^*)d>0$ for all nonzero $d$ in the critical cone $C(x^*,\lambda^*)$, then $x^*$ is a strict local minimizer."),
  1203: (THM, r"<b>Bordered Hessian test.</b> For $\min f$ s.t. $h(x)=0$ with $m$ constraints and Jacobian $J=\nabla h(x^*)^\top$, form $B=\begin{bmatrix}0&J\\J^\top&\nabla^2_{xx}L\end{bmatrix}$. If the last $n-m$ leading principal minors of $B$ all have sign $(-1)^m$, then $\nabla^2_{xx}L\succ0$ on $M$ and $x^*$ is a strict local minimizer."),
  1204: (DEF, r"The <b>critical cone</b> is $C(x^*,\lambda^*)=\{d\in\mathcal F(x^*): \nabla c_i(x^*)^\top d=0$ for all active inequalities with $\lambda_i^*>0\}$. Under strict complementarity ($\lambda_i^*>0$ for every active inequality) it equals the subspace $M$; otherwise it is a polyhedral cone."),
  1205: (PROP, r"On a curved constraint surface the relevant curvature is that of the Lagrangian, $\nabla^2_{xx}L=\nabla^2f+\sum_i\lambda_i\nabla^2c_i$, restricted to $M$, not $\nabla^2f$ alone: the term $\sum\lambda_i\nabla^2c_i$ accounts for the bending of the surface."),
  1206: (EX, r"Worked bordered-Hessian check: at the KKT point of $\min f$ subject to one equality constraint ($m=1$, $n=2$), the sign of $\det B$ must equal $(-1)^m=-1$; here $\det B<0$, confirming a strict local minimum."),
  # ---- 13 · Projection ----
  1301: (THM, r"<b>Projection theorem.</b> Let $C\subset\mathbb R^n$ be nonempty, closed and convex. For every $p$ there is a unique $P_C(p)=\arg\min_{x\in C}\|x-p\|$, characterised by the variational inequality $(p-P_C(p))^\top(y-P_C(p))\le0$ for all $y\in C$."),
  1302: (PROP, r"For closed convex $C$, the projection is <b>firmly non-expansive</b>: $\|P(p)-P(q)\|^2\le(P(p)-P(q))^\top(p-q)$, and hence non-expansive: $\|P(p)-P(q)\|\le\|p-q\|$ for all $p,q$."),
  1303: (PROP, r"For the hyperplane $H=\{x:a^\top x=b\}$, $P_H(p)=p-\dfrac{a^\top p-b}{\|a\|^2}a$. For the halfspace $\{x:a^\top x\le b\}$, $P(p)=p-\dfrac{\max\{0,a^\top p-b\}}{\|a\|^2}a$."),
  1307: (PROP, r"For the box $[l,u]$, $P(p)_i=\min\{u_i,\max\{l_i,p_i\}\}$. For the ball $\{x:\|x-c\|\le r\}$, $P(p)=c+\dfrac{r}{\max\{r,\|p-c\|\}}(p-c)$."),
  1304: (THM, r"<b>Moreau decomposition.</b> Let $K$ be a closed convex cone with polar $K^\circ$. Every $x$ can be written uniquely as $x=P_K(x)+P_{K^\circ}(x)$ with $P_K(x)^\top P_{K^\circ}(x)=0$."),
  1305: (ALG, r"<b>Dykstra's algorithm</b> (projection onto $C_1\cap C_2$, both closed convex). Set $x_0=p$, $p_0=q_0=0$. Repeat: $y_k=P_{C_1}(x_k+p_k)$, $p_{k+1}=x_k+p_k-y_k$, $x_{k+1}=P_{C_2}(y_k+q_k)$, $q_{k+1}=y_k+q_k-x_{k+1}$. Then $x_k\to P_{C_1\cap C_2}(p)$."),
  1306: (EX, r"Projection onto the simplex $\Delta=\{x\ge0,\ \mathbf 1^\top x=1\}$: $P_\Delta(p)_i=\max\{p_i-\tau,0\}$, where $\tau$ is chosen so that $\sum_i\max\{p_i-\tau,0\}=1$."),
  # ---- 14 · Gradient projection ----
  1401: (ALG, r"<b>Projected gradient.</b> For $\min_{x\in C}f(x)$ with $C$ closed convex: $x_{k+1}=P_C\big(x_k-\alpha_k\nabla f(x_k)\big)$, with $\alpha_k$ constant (e.g. $1/L$) or chosen by a line search."),
  1402: (THM, r"For convex closed $C$ and any $\alpha>0$, $x^*$ satisfies the first-order condition $\nabla f(x^*)^\top(x-x^*)\ge0\ \forall x\in C$ if and only if $x^*=P_C\big(x^*-\alpha\nabla f(x^*)\big)$."),
  1403: (THM, r"If $f$ is convex with $L$-Lipschitz gradient, projected gradient with $\alpha=1/L$ gives $f(x_k)-f^*\le\dfrac{L\|x_0-x^*\|^2}{2k}$; if $f$ is also $\mu$-strongly convex, $\|x_k-x^*\|\le(1-\mu/L)^{k/2}\|x_0-x^*\|$ (linear convergence)."),
  1404: (ALG, r"<b>Armijo along the projection arc.</b> For trial steps $\alpha=\bar\alpha\rho^m$, let $x(\alpha)=P_C(x_k-\alpha\nabla f(x_k))$; accept the first $\alpha$ with $f(x(\alpha))\le f(x_k)+c_1\nabla f(x_k)^\top(x(\alpha)-x_k)$."),
  1405: (DEF, r"For nonlinear equality constraints $h(x)=0$, a step along the tangent plane leaves the surface; a <b>restoration step</b> returns to it, e.g. $x\leftarrow x-\nabla h(x)^\top\big(\nabla h(x)\nabla h(x)^\top\big)^{-1}h(x)$ (Gauss–Newton step on $h(x)=0$)."),
  1406: (EX, r"On $C=\{x\ge0\}$, $P_C(p)=\max(p,0)$ componentwise, so $x_{k+1}=\max\big(x_k-\alpha\nabla f(x_k),0\big)$: coordinates pushed negative are clamped to $0$."),
  # ---- 15 · Active set ----
  1501: (ALG, r"<b>Active-set method for convex QP</b> $\min\tfrac12x^\top Gx+c^\top x$ s.t. $a_i^\top x\ge b_i$. Keep a working set $W_k$; compute $p_k$ from the equality QP $\min_p\tfrac12p^\top Gp+g_k^\top p$ s.t. $a_i^\top p=0$, $i\in W_k$."),
  1502: (PROP, r"If $p_k\ne0$, take $\alpha_k=\min\Big\{1,\ \min_{i\notin W_k,\ a_i^\top p_k<0}\dfrac{b_i-a_i^\top x_k}{a_i^\top p_k}\Big\}$; if $\alpha_k<1$ the minimizing (blocking) constraint is added to $W_k$."),
  1503: (PROP, r"If $p_k=0$, compute the multipliers $\hat\lambda_i$ of the equality QP. If all $\hat\lambda_i\ge0$ for inequality constraints in $W_k$, $x_k$ is optimal (KKT holds); otherwise remove a constraint with $\hat\lambda_j<0$ (usually the most negative) from $W_k$."),
  1504: (THM, r"If $G\succ0$ and every step with $\alpha_k>0$ strictly decreases the objective, the active-set method terminates in finitely many iterations, since no working set can recur and there are finitely many working sets."),
  1505: (DEF, r"A point is <b>degenerate</b> if the active constraint gradients are linearly dependent (e.g. more than $n$ active constraints). Then a step with $\alpha_k=0$ may occur, the working set changes without progress, and cycling is possible; anti-cycling rules (e.g. Bland's rule, perturbation) prevent it."),
  1506: (EX, r"Worked 2-D active-set run: starting feasible, the method slides along a wall, is blocked by a second constraint, drops a constraint with a negative multiplier, and terminates at $x^*=(2,2)$ where all working-set multipliers are positive."),
  # ---- 16 · Lagrangian duality ----
  1601: (DEF, r"For $\min f(x)$ s.t. $g(x)\le0$, the <b>primal (perturbation) function</b> is $\omega(z)=\inf\{f(x): g(x)\le z\}$. The dual value equals the intercept at $z=0$ of the best non-vertical hyperplane supporting $\omega$ from below: $q(\mu)=\inf_z\{\omega(z)+\mu^\top z\}$."),
  1602: (THM, r"<b>Weak duality.</b> With Lagrangian $L(x,\lambda,\mu)=f(x)+\lambda^\top h(x)+\mu^\top g(x)$ and dual function $q(\lambda,\mu)=\inf_xL(x,\lambda,\mu)$: for every feasible $x$ and every $\mu\ge0$, $q(\lambda,\mu)\le f(x)$. Hence $d^*\le p^*$; $p^*-d^*$ is the duality gap."),
  1603: (THM, r"<b>Strong duality.</b> If the problem is convex and Slater's condition holds, then $d^*=p^*$ and the dual optimum is attained; $(x^*,\lambda^*,\mu^*)$ are primal-dual optimal iff they satisfy the KKT conditions."),
  1604: (PROP, r"The dual function $q(\lambda,\mu)=\inf_xL(x,\lambda,\mu)$ is concave (as a pointwise infimum of functions affine in $(\lambda,\mu)$), regardless of convexity of $f$, $g$, $h$."),
  1605: (THM, r"<b>Sensitivity.</b> Under strong duality and differentiability of $\omega$ at $0$, the optimal multipliers satisfy $\mu_j^*=-\dfrac{\partial\omega}{\partial z_j}(0)$: relaxing constraint $j$ by $\delta$ lowers the optimal value by about $\mu_j^*\delta$."),
  1606: (DEF, r"<b>Hard-margin SVM.</b> Given data $(x_i,y_i)$, $y_i\in\{\pm1\}$: $\min_{w,b}\tfrac12\|w\|^2$ s.t. $y_i(w^\top x_i+b)\ge1$; the margin width is $2/\|w\|$. Lagrangian: $L=\tfrac12\|w\|^2-\sum_i\alpha_i\big(y_i(w^\top x_i+b)-1\big)$, $\alpha\ge0$."),
  1608: (THM, r"<b>SVM dual.</b> $\max_{\alpha\ge0}\sum_i\alpha_i-\tfrac12\sum_{i,j}\alpha_i\alpha_jy_iy_jx_i^\top x_j$ s.t. $\sum_i\alpha_iy_i=0$; then $w=\sum_i\alpha_iy_ix_i$, and $\alpha_i>0$ only for support vectors. Replacing $x_i^\top x_j$ by a kernel $k(x_i,x_j)$ gives the kernel SVM."),
  # ---- 17 · Wolfe duality ----
  1701: (DEF, r"<b>Wolfe dual</b> of the convex program $\min f(x)$ s.t. $g(x)\le0$: $\max_{x,\mu}L(x,\mu)=f(x)+\mu^\top g(x)$ s.t. $\nabla_xL(x,\mu)=0$, $\mu\ge0$."),
  1702: (THM, r"<b>Wolfe duality.</b> If $f,g$ are convex and $C^1$, $x^*$ solves the primal and a constraint qualification holds, then there is $\mu^*$ such that $(x^*,\mu^*)$ solves the Wolfe dual and the optimal values coincide."),
  1703: (EX, r"For the QP $\min\tfrac12x^\top Qx+c^\top x$ s.t. $Ax\ge b$, $Q\succ0$, the Wolfe dual is $\max_{x,\lambda}-\tfrac12x^\top Qx+b^\top\lambda$ s.t. $Qx+c-A^\top\lambda=0$, $\lambda\ge0$; eliminating $x$ gives $\max_{\lambda\ge0}-\tfrac12(A^\top\lambda-c)^\top Q^{-1}(A^\top\lambda-c)+b^\top\lambda$."),
  1704: (PROP, r"Without convexity, $\nabla_xL(x,\mu)=0$ does not imply $x$ minimizes $L(\cdot,\mu)$ (it may be a maximizer or saddle), so the Wolfe dual can fail weak duality and its value may exceed $p^*$."),
  1705: (ALG, r"<b>Dual subgradient method.</b> Given $\mu_k\ge0$, compute $x_k\in\arg\min_xL(x,\mu_k)$; then $g(x_k)$ is a subgradient of $q$ at $\mu_k$; update $\mu_{k+1}=\max\{0,\ \mu_k+t_kg(x_k)\}$ with step sizes $t_k>0$."),
  1706: (EX, r"<b>Maximum entropy.</b> $\max-\sum_ip_i\log p_i$ s.t. $\sum_ip_i=1$, $\sum_ip_iE_i=\bar E$. Stationarity of the Lagrangian gives the Boltzmann form $p_i=e^{-\beta E_i}/\sum_je^{-\beta E_j}$, with $\beta$ the multiplier of the energy constraint."),
  # ---- 18 · LP ----
  1801: (DEF, r"<b>Standard-form LP and its dual.</b> Primal: $\min c^\top x$ s.t. $Ax=b$, $x\ge0$. Dual: $\max b^\top y$ s.t. $A^\top y\le c$."),
  1807: (DEF, r"A <b>basic feasible solution</b> of $Ax=b$, $x\ge0$ ($A$ of rank $m$) is a feasible $x$ whose nonzero components correspond to linearly independent columns of $A$. <b>Theorem.</b> $x$ is a basic feasible solution iff it is a vertex (extreme point) of the feasible polyhedron."),
  1802: (THM, r"<b>Fundamental theorem of LP.</b> If the feasible set is nonempty, there is a basic feasible solution; if the LP has an optimal solution, it has an optimal basic feasible solution (an optimal vertex)."),
  1803: (THM, r"<b>Complementary slackness.</b> Feasible $x$ and $y$ are optimal for the primal and dual iff $x_j(c_j-a_j^\top y)=0$ for all $j$ (and, for inequality-form primals, $y_i(a_i^\top x-b_i)=0$ for all $i$)."),
  1804: (THM, r"<b>LP duality.</b> If either problem has a finite optimum, so does the other, and $c^\top x^*=b^\top y^*$. If the primal is infeasible, the dual is either infeasible or unbounded; via Farkas, a certificate $y$ with $A^\top y\le0$, $b^\top y>0$ makes the dual unbounded whenever it is feasible."),
  1805: (ALG, r"<b>Simplex method.</b> Move between adjacent basic feasible solutions; at each step compute reduced costs $\bar c_j=c_j-a_j^\top y$; if all $\bar c_j\ge0$ the current vertex is optimal; otherwise bring in a variable with $\bar c_j<0$ and pivot out the variable chosen by the minimum-ratio test (unbounded if no ratio exists)."),
  1808: (DEF, r"<b>Central path.</b> For $\mu>0$, $(x(\mu),y(\mu),s(\mu))$ solves $Ax=b$, $A^\top y+s=c$, $x_is_i=\mu$ for all $i$, $x,s>0$. Interior-point methods follow this path as $\mu\to0$, where it converges to a primal-dual optimal pair."),
  1806: (EX, r"Primal–dual equality on a small LP: solve the primal graphically (optimal vertex) and the dual graphically; the two optimal values are equal, $c^\top x^*=b^\top y^*$, as LP duality guarantees."),
  # ---- 19 · Penalty & barrier ----
  1901: (DEF, r"<b>Quadratic penalty.</b> For $\min f(x)$ s.t. $h(x)=0$, $g(x)\le0$: $Q(x;c)=f(x)+\tfrac c2\big(\|h(x)\|^2+\|\max(0,g(x))\|^2\big)$. <b>Theorem.</b> If $x_k$ globally minimizes $Q(\cdot;c_k)$ and $c_k\to\infty$, every limit point of $\{x_k\}$ solves the constrained problem."),
  1902: (DEF, r"<b>Log barrier.</b> For $\min f(x)$ s.t. $g(x)<0$ (strictly feasible interior): $B(x;\mu)=f(x)-\mu\sum_j\log(-g_j(x))$; inverse barrier: $f(x)-\mu\sum_j1/g_j(x)$. As $\mu\downarrow0$ the minimizers $x(\mu)$ approach a solution of the constrained problem from the interior."),
  1903: (PROP, r"The Hessian of the quadratic penalty, $\nabla^2Q=\nabla^2f+c\sum_ih_i\nabla^2h_i+c\,\nabla h^\top\nabla h$, has $m$ eigenvalues of order $c$ and the rest of order $1$, so its condition number grows like $c$ as $c\to\infty$."),
  1904: (PROP, r"If $x_k$ minimizes $Q(\cdot;c_k)$ and $x_k\to x^*$ (a regular point), then $\lambda_k=c_kh(x_k)\to\lambda^*$, the Lagrange multiplier at $x^*$."),
  1905: (ALG, r"<b>Augmented Lagrangian (method of multipliers).</b> $L_c(x,\lambda)=f(x)+\lambda^\top h(x)+\tfrac c2\|h(x)\|^2$. Repeat: $x_k\in\arg\min_xL_{c_k}(x,\lambda_k)$; $\lambda_{k+1}=\lambda_k+c_kh(x_k)$. Convergence holds for finite (moderately large) $c$, avoiding the ill-conditioning of $c\to\infty$."),
  1906: (EX, r"Worked augmented-Lagrangian run: with fixed $c$, the update $\lambda_{k+1}=\lambda_k+c\,h(x_k)$ drives $\lambda_k$ to $\lambda^*$ in a few outer iterations while $h(x_k)\to0$."),
}

import re
def _lt(t):  # a bare "<" inside math would be read as an HTML tag
    return re.sub(r"\$[^$]*\$", lambda m: m.group(0).replace("<", r"\lt ").replace(">", r"\gt "), t)

FIX = {cid: {"stmtKind": k, "statement": _lt(t)} for cid, (k, t) in ST.items()}
